  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 330 PM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nWednesday, a strong 594-dam 500-mb upper-level ridge will remain \nanchored across the southern United States. Southerly low-level flow \nand warm air advection (WAA) will support temperatures climbing well \nabove normal, with highs in the middle 90s to lower 100s. By late \nWednesday afternoon into the evening, a weak shortwave disturbance \nwill move into the region along the northern periphery of the ridge, \nproviding sufficient lift for increasing shower and thunderstorm \nchances. Current model guidance suggests convection will develop \nacross western Nebraska and the Panhandle before moving east-\nsoutheast into the forecast area Wednesday night and into Thursday \nmorning, which is a typical evolution in this pattern. At this time, \nthe severe weather threat appears low as the strongest mid- and \nupper-level forcing and deep-layer shear remain north of the region. \nWidespread wetting rainfall is also not expected with this initial \nround of convection, as storms are forecast to remain scattered. \nOvernight lows will remain mild, generally in the middle 60s.\n\nThursday, the upper-level ridge is forecast to strengthen to around \n597 dam while shifting into the southwestern United States. This \nwill establish northwest flow aloft across the central Plains, \ntransitioning the region into a classic ring of fire pattern. Weak \ndisturbances moving along the northern periphery of the ridge will \ncontinue to provide opportunities for showers and thunderstorms. \nHigh temperatures remain somewhat uncertain owing to differences in \ncloud cover and convective evolution among the guidance, though \nhighs in the lower 90s currently appear reasonable. By Thursday \nevening, model guidance suggests another round of convection \ndeveloping across South Dakota before moving southeast into portions \nof the forecast area. This currently appears to be the best \nopportunity for more widespread wetting rainfall during the forecast \nperiod, though confidence in the exact timing and placement remains \nlow. Forecast trends will continue to be monitored over the next \nseveral forecast cycles. Overnight lows Thursday night will once \nagain remain mild, generally in the middle to upper 60s.\n\nFriday through the weekend, northwest flow aloft is expected to \npersist as the upper-level ridge remains anchored across the \nsouthwestern United States. This pattern will allow temperatures to \nmoderate closer to seasonal normals, with afternoon highs generally \nin the upper 80s to lower 90s. Low precipitation chances will \npersist as occasional weak disturbances move through the northwest \nflow, though increasing subsidence beneath the ridge should limit \nconvective coverage. Fire weather concerns are expected to remain \nlow through the period, with minimum relative humidity values \ngenerally remaining above 30 percent and synoptic winds staying \nrelatively light. Overall, the pattern favors warm temperatures with \nperiodic, mainly low-end precipitation chances, while the best \nopportunities for rainfall currently appear to be Wednesday night \nand again Thursday night.",
